  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 215 AM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nLooking forward to the mid-week there is a low-pressure system that \nis forecast to impact our area Wednesday. Global model ensemble \nguidance is in good agreement that an extended area of mid-level \ntroughing will move off the Rockies Tuesday night into Wednesday \nmorning. Within the trough an interaction of two short waves leads \nto a negatively oriented trough axis running through the Southeast \nand down the Florida Peninsula. There is also good agreement between \nthe members that a surface low will develop in the Gulf waters \nsomewhere west of Florida, but this is where we begin to see \ndivergence among ensemble guidance. Preceding this setup, the \nensemble mean for precipitable water will approach 2 inches which \nis well above the 90th percentile for this time of year and would \nput it in the ballpark of the maximum observed values. From the \nStorm Prediction Center (SPC) ensemble cluster analysis, two \ngeneral scenarios emerge for South Florida.\n\nOne of these scenarios has the mid-level trough swing over Florida \nquickly and the developing surface low does not reach its deepest \npoint until after it has moved off to the east of South Florida. \nThese scenarios have lower precipitation totals than the slower \nsolutions but depending on the strength of the shortwave, this setup \ncould produce a better shear profile for severe storms. A more \nnortherly track of the surface low would further increases this \npotential as the optimal sector of the storm for severe storm \ndevelopment would move closer to South Florida. These faster \nsolutions bring a frontal boundary over the area by early Thursday \nmorning.\n\nAnother of these scenarios points to a higher rainfall solution for \nSouth Florida on Wednesday due to two different reasons. Either a \nslower propagating surface low, or a slightly faster propagating low \nthat forms further to the south west near the Yucatan Channel. Both \nof these scenarios give more time for the surface low to mature \nbefore reaching our area and allow for more moisture advection ahead \nof the system. It is these scenarios that produce the highest \nprecipitable water values for Wednesday and point to the elevated \nrainfall totals compared to the faster moving storm scenarios. Early \nindications have rainfall amounts of 2-4 inches for the eastern \nmetros with the possibility of localized areas receiving higher \namounts. \n\nThe impacts of the increased rainfall Wednesday would coincide with \na return of moderate flooding thresholds due to the return of the \nking tide so partners at risk of coastal flooding should monitor the \ndevelopment of this system over the next two days. Following the \nlow, a frontal boundary will bring drier air over South Florida no \nlater than Thursday night and dew points will fall to the mid 50s as \nwe move into the weekend.",
